[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Wildcards in Java Generics: Upper Bounded, Lower Bounded, and Unbounded"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java generics provide a powerful way to write type-safe code that can work with different types. Wildcards enhance the flexibility of generics by allowing you to specify constraints on the types that can be used with a generic type. This tutorial explores upper bounded, lower bounded, and unbounded wildcards with detailed explanations and examples."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Unbounded Wildcards"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An unbounded wildcard is represented by `?`. It means that the generic type can be of any type. It's useful when you want to write code that works with a generic type regardless of its specific type parameter.  Often used when the method implementation doesn't depend on the type parameter."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.List;

public class UnboundedWildcardExample {

    public static void printList(List<?> list) {
        for (Object element : list) {
            System.out.println(element);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> integerList = List.of(1, 2, 3);
        List<String> stringList = List.of("Hello", "World");

        printList(integerList);
        printList(stringList);
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, `printList` accepts a `List` of any type because of the unbounded wildcard `<?>`. The method iterates through the list and prints each element.  Because the type is unknown, the elements are treated as `Object` instances."},{"order":7,"type":"heading","text":"2. Upper Bounded Wildcards"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"An upper bounded wildcard restricts the unknown type to be a specific type or a subtype of that type. It is denoted by `? extends Type`. This is useful when you want to work with a specific type hierarchy."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.List;

class Animal {
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Dog is eating");
    }
    public void bark() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Cat is eating");
    }
    public void meow() {
        System.out.println("Meow!");
    }
}

public class UpperBoundedWildcardExample {

    public static void feedAnimals(List<? extends Animal> animals) {
        for (Animal animal : animals) {
            animal.eat();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Dog> dogs = List.of(new Dog(), new Dog());
        List<Cat> cats = List.of(new Cat(), new Cat());

        feedAnimals(dogs);
        feedAnimals(cats);
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `feedAnimals` accepts a `List` of `Animal` or any subtype of `Animal` (like `Dog` and `Cat`). The method then calls the `eat()` method on each animal.  The upper bound ensures that the `eat()` method is available on all elements of the list."},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Important: With upper-bounded wildcards, you cannot add elements to the list inside the method because the compiler cannot guarantee the exact type of the list.  It only knows that it's some subtype of `Animal`, but not which specific subtype."},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"3. Lower Bounded Wildcards"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"A lower bounded wildcard restricts the unknown type to be a specific type or a supertype of that type. It is denoted by `? super Type`. This is useful when you want to write data to a generic type, and you need to ensure that the data is compatible with the type or its supertypes."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Grandparent {}
class Parent extends Grandparent {}
class Child extends Parent {}

public class LowerBoundedWildcardExample {

    public static void addChildren(List<? super Child> list) {
        list.add(new Child());
        list.add(new Child());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Child> childList = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Parent> parentList = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Grandparent> grandparentList = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Object> objectList = new ArrayList<>();

        addChildren(childList);
        addChildren(parentList);
        addChildren(grandparentList);
        addChildren(objectList);

        System.out.println("Child List: " + childList);
        System.out.println("Parent List: " + parentList);
        System.out.println("Grandparent List: " + grandparentList);
        System.out.println("Object List: " + objectList);
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `addChildren` accepts a `List` of `Child` or any supertype of `Child` (like `Parent`, `Grandparent`, or `Object`). The method adds two `Child` objects to the list. This is safe because any list that can hold a `Child`'s supertype can also hold a `Child`."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"With lower-bounded wildcards, you can add elements of type `Type` (or subtypes of `Type`) to the list. However, reading from the list is more restricted. You can only be sure that you'll get an `Object` back, as that's the common supertype of all Java objects."},{"order":17,"type":"heading","text":"Summary"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Unbounded Wildcard (`<?>`):** Represents an unknown type. Useful when the method doesn't depend on the specific type parameter."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Upper Bounded Wildcard (`? extends Type`):** Represents a type that is `Type` or a subtype of `Type`.  Useful for reading data from a generic type hierarchy."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Lower Bounded Wildcard (`? super Type`):** Represents a type that is `Type` or a supertype of `Type`. Useful for writing data to a generic type hierarchy."},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding and utilizing wildcards effectively is crucial for writing flexible and type-safe generic code in Java. Choose the appropriate wildcard based on whether you need to read from, write to, or simply process a generic type without needing to know its specific type parameter."}]
